# SpreadsheetBench full_verified — committed run configuration (see ci/benchmarks/lib/load_overrides.sh).
#
# THE DATASET. This tier evaluates the VERIFIED 400-task re-release, not the original 912-task
# set that `full` runs. ci_setup.sh maps TIER=full_verified to SPREADSHEETBENCH_VARIANT=verified_400,
# which is a separate download with corrected instructions and golden files — NOT a subset of
# the 912 archive. See ci/benchmarks/spreadsheetbench/fetch_data.sh for what actually differs.
#
# SCORING. Report the benchmark's HARD score: a task counts only when EVERY graded test case
# matches. On this dataset that is a formality rather than a restriction — the verified release
# ships ONE graded case per task, so hard and soft are identical by construction (matches/1 is
# either 0 or 1). It is set explicitly anyway for two reasons:
#   1. the reported metric name stays the same across `full` and `full_verified`, so the two tiers'
#      numbers can be put in the same column, and
#   2. if a future release restores multiple cases per task, this tier keeps reporting the
#      strict metric instead of silently loosening to partial credit.
#
# Note the corollary, which is the good news: because soft == hard here, a full_verified result is
# directly comparable to published "native hard score" figures with no caveat about which of
# the two metrics is being quoted. On `full` that caveat is load-bearing (soft >= hard by
# construction, and the same champion measured 63.4% soft vs 56.0% hard on val).
#
# NB Bernoulli per-task reward makes the gate's SE wider than under soft. Pair this with a
# lower `gate_k_se` dispatch input (0.2), exactly as for `full` — gate_k_se cannot live in
# this file, because the workflow always sets GATE_K_SE and the environment deliberately wins
# over committed overrides.
SB_SCORING=hard

# NO WARM START. Deliberately absent (contrast pilot/overrides.env, which sets SB_WARM_SEED=1).
# This is a comparison tier: its baseline must be the pristine seed capability so the
# base->opt delta it publishes is a genuine from-scratch result, not the continuation of an
# earlier run's champion.

# ===========================================================================================
# TEMPORARY — DO NOT MERGE. This branch exists only to take ONE no-skill control measurement
# in CI, and is discarded afterwards. See the PR that carries it, and issue #538.
#
# WHY IT IS ON full_verified RATHER THAN A NEW TIER. A control is only a control if it differs
# from the thing it controls in exactly ONE respect. Setting the flag on THIS tier means the
# control is measured on literally the same dataset (verified_400), the same committed 80/40/280
# split, the same 30-turn agent budget, the same 8-way container concurrency and the same `hard`
# scoring — not on a copy of them that could drift.
#
# WHAT WE WANT OUT OF THE RUN: the `finalize_baseline` number — the BLANKED capability on the
# 280 sealed test tasks. Everything else the run produces is incidental.
#
# WHY WE WANT IT. Run 36175707483 scored 0.764 held-out for Gemma-4-31B-It against a SEED
# baseline of 0.632, while WikiSkill (arXiv 2608.27454v1) reports 68.0 for the same model
# against a NO-SKILL baseline of 48.3. Our seed is a tuned prompt plus a task template and
# already scores near SkillOpt's 63.1, so our +13.2 delta is NOT comparable to their +19.7.
# This supplies the missing anchor.
#
# WHAT "no skill" MEANS HERE: blanks prompt.md in the run's WORK COPY (never the repo's
# seed_capability/), and the adapter then sends NO system message rather than an empty one. The
# built-in _TASK_TEMPLATE still supplies the task framing, so this is "no skill", not "no prompt
# of any kind" — the closest analogue to the paper's "benchmark's default system prompt" without
# being identical to it. Say so when quoting the number.
SB_EMPTY_SEED=1
# ===========================================================================================
